window.CPACC_DOMAIN_4_LESSON_01 = {
  "title": "Assistive Technology Foundations",  "libraryTopics": ["Assistive Technology", "Assistive Technology Compatibility", "Functional Solutions Model", "Alternative Input", "Cognitive Supports", "Digital Accessibility"],
  "time": "30 to 40 minutes",
  "parts": [
    {
      "title": "Lesson Overview",
      "body": "Assistive technology, often shortened to Assistive Technology (AT), refers to the devices, software, equipment, services, features, supports, and strategies that people with disabilities use to perform tasks, communicate, learn, work, travel, manage daily life, and participate in society. In professional accessibility practice, AT should never be treated as a separate island floating beside accessibility. It is part of a larger ecosystem that includes accessible design, inclusive policy, procurement decisions, training, support services, user skill, organizational culture, and the physical, digital, and communication environments where participation occurs.\n\nA strong accessibility professional understands that AT and accessible design are partners, not substitutes. Accessible design removes barriers from the environment so that more people can use products, services, and spaces directly. AT helps an individual interact with information, technology, or the built environment in a way that fits that person's body, senses, movement, cognition, communication, learning style, and preferences. When the environment is well designed, AT can provide efficient, dignified access. When the environment is poorly designed, even excellent AT can be blocked by missing structure, unlabeled controls, inaccessible documents, poor communication practices, physical barriers, or organizational processes that were never designed with disabled people in mind.\n\nThis distinction matters because organizations often misunderstand where responsibility belongs. A workplace may purchase screen reader software and then believe it has solved accessibility for blind employees. A school may provide captions for one video but continue using inaccessible scanned documents. A healthcare provider may offer a patient portal without considering keyboard access, plain language instructions, or compatibility with mobile accessibility features. In each case, AT may be present, but participation is still limited because the surrounding system has not been designed accessibly. The professional question is not simply whether a person has technology. The deeper question is whether the environment gives that technology the information, controls, timing, formats, and support required for equal participation.\n\nDomain 4 begins by building this systems view. Learners will examine categories of AT, but the goal is not to memorize a device catalog. Device lists become outdated quickly. Professional reasoning lasts longer. Accessibility consultants, program managers, educators, designers, developers, auditors, procurement teams, and policy writers need to understand why people use different tools, what those tools need from the environment, how barriers appear in real tasks, and how organizations can reduce dependence on individual workarounds. A screen reader, refreshable braille display, screen magnifier, switch system, eye tracking system, speech recognition tool, captioning service, hearing technology, reading support, reminder system, or Augmentative and Alternative Communication (AAC) device is not merely a product category. Each represents a way a person may access information, express intent, control a task, manage attention, process language, or communicate with others.\n\nThe lesson also introduces a recurring theme for the rest of the domain: AT use is highly individual. Two people with the same disability label may use very different tools. One blind professional may rely heavily on speech output, while another may depend on braille for editing, spelling, code, or complex tables. One person with low vision may prefer magnification, while another may use high contrast, screen reader output, or a combination of visual and nonvisual strategies. One person with limited hand movement may use speech recognition for writing but keyboard shortcuts for editing. A person with a cognitive disability may use reminders, simplified layouts, text to speech, visual schedules, or plain language supports. The accessibility professional must avoid the tidy trap of assuming that one disability equals one technology. Real users are more interesting than that, and professional practice must leave room for variation.\n\nBy the end of this lesson, you should be able to explain AT as part of functional access, describe its relationship to accessible design, recognize why inaccessible environments can block otherwise effective technology, and begin analyzing AT issues from the perspective of user goals, task demands, environmental barriers, and organizational responsibility. That reasoning is more valuable than simply naming tools. The Certified Professional in Accessibility Core Competencies (CPACC) examination expects candidates to understand AT concepts, but professional practice requires something broader: the ability to see how technology, people, environments, and decisions interact. That is the starting point this domain opens."
    },
    {
      "title": "Opening Scenario",
      "body": "A new employee joins an organization as a project coordinator. The employee is blind and uses a screen reader, a refreshable braille display, and keyboard commands to complete work tasks. During onboarding, the organization proudly explains that it has provided the employee's requested technology and that the information technology team has installed it correctly. From the organization's point of view, the accommodation appears complete. The employee has the software, the device is connected, and the computer starts without errors.\n\nThen real work begins. The employee opens the organization's project management application to review assigned tasks. The dashboard contains several buttons that only announce as 'button.' The employee cannot tell which button creates a task, which one opens filters, and which one changes the project view. A menu opens from the keyboard, but focus becomes trapped inside it, so the employee cannot return to the task list without refreshing the page. A form for updating project status includes required fields, but the required status is only shown visually with red text. When the employee submits the form with missing information, an error message appears near the top of the page, but keyboard focus does not move there and the screen reader does not announce it. The employee hears that something failed but receives no usable path to correction.\n\nThe employee then opens a training document in Portable Document Format (PDF). The document looks polished to sighted employees, but it is an image-based scan with no selectable text. The screen reader cannot read the content because the document has not been processed with Optical Character Recognition (OCR), and even after OCR, the reading order may still be unreliable if headings, lists, tables, and form fields are not properly structured. Later, during a video orientation, captions are present, but they are inaccurate and omit several important names, dates, and instructions. The problem is not limited to blindness. A Deaf employee who depends on accurate captions would also be affected. In this sentence, Deaf with a capital D refers to cultural and linguistic identity, while deaf with a lowercase d refers more generally to hearing status. That distinction must be explained in text because capitalization alone may not be announced by screen readers or obvious in braille depending on settings and context.\n\nWhat the organization saw was a successful technology installation. What the user experienced was a chain of barriers created by inaccessible design, inaccessible documents, incomplete media access, and weak organizational review. The screen reader worked. The braille display worked. The keyboard worked. The employee's skills were not the problem. The barriers came from missing labels, poor focus management, inaccessible error handling, unstructured documents, and inaccurate communication access.\n\nAn accessibility consultant would not respond by asking whether the employee needs a different screen reader. That might be relevant in rare cases, but it is not the first professional question. The stronger investigation asks what task the employee is trying to complete, what information the interface provides visually, whether that same information is available programmatically, whether all controls can be reached and operated from the keyboard, whether errors are identified and described accessibly, whether documents contain real text and meaningful structure, and whether video content provides accurate synchronized captions and transcripts where appropriate. The consultant also asks why these barriers reached the employee in the first place. Was accessibility included in procurement? Did the organization request an Accessibility Conformance Report (ACR)? Did anyone validate the vendor's claims? Were documents created from accessible source files? Did training teams know how to check captions? Did quality assurance include keyboard and screen reader testing?\n\nThis scenario demonstrates the central lesson of Domain 4. Assistive technology can be essential, powerful, and life changing, but it cannot turn inaccessible systems into accessible ones by itself. When organizations treat AT as the whole solution, disabled users are forced to absorb the cost of every design failure. They spend time guessing, troubleshooting, asking for help, documenting barriers, and explaining problems that should have been prevented. Professional accessibility practice shifts that burden back where it belongs: into design, procurement, development, documentation, training, testing, support, and continuous improvement. The goal is not to admire the user's persistence. The goal is to build environments where persistence is not required just to perform ordinary tasks."
    },
    {
      "title": "Core Learning",
      "body": "Assistive technology works by receiving, transforming, presenting, or sending information in ways that support a user's functional needs. Some AT changes how information is perceived. A screen reader converts text and interface information into speech or braille. A screen magnifier enlarges content or changes contrast. Captions convert spoken audio into text. Some AT changes how a person controls a task. Switch access, alternative keyboards, eye tracking, voice control, and sip-and-puff systems can replace or supplement a mouse, touch gesture, or standard keyboard. Some AT supports communication, memory, reading, attention, organization, or comprehension. Augmentative and Alternative Communication (AAC) systems, text-to-speech tools, reminders, reading guides, symbol supports, simplified displays, and task prompts may all function as AT depending on the user's needs and context.\n\nThe most important professional concept is that AT usually depends on information supplied by the environment. A screen reader does not invent a meaningful button name when a developer fails to provide one. It can only announce the accessible name exposed by the interface, guess from surrounding text in limited situations, or leave the user with something vague such as 'button.' A refreshable braille display cannot present the structure of a table if the document is only a picture of a table. Speech recognition software works more reliably when visible labels and programmatic names match, because users need to say commands that correspond to what they can perceive. Switch users depend on predictable focus order because moving through every interactive element one step at a time becomes exhausting when focus jumps, disappears, or enters traps. Captions and Communication Access Realtime Translation (CART) depend on spoken content being accurately represented in text at the right time. Each technology has different mechanics, but the pattern is consistent: AT needs accessible information and operable interaction.\n\nThis is why accessibility professionals distinguish between user technology and environmental accessibility. If a blind user cannot complete a checkout form because required fields are not announced, the root problem is not blindness and it is not the screen reader. The root problem is that the form does not communicate required status and error recovery accessibly. If a person using speech recognition cannot activate a visible 'Submit Request' button by saying 'click Submit Request,' the issue may be a mismatch between the visible label and the accessible name. If a person using keyboard-only navigation cannot close a dialog, the issue may be focus management, keyboard event handling, or a missing close control. If a person who depends on captions misses safety instructions during training, the issue may be caption accuracy, synchronization, or the absence of a transcript. Professional analysis looks past the surface symptom and asks what information, control, timing, or feedback failed.\n\nAT also exists across a wide range of environments. Digital accessibility often receives attention because software barriers are common, but AT is not limited to websites and applications. A person may use a mobility device in a building, a hearing loop in a meeting room, tactile markings in transit, a communication device in healthcare, screen magnification in a classroom, a braille note taker at work, or a reminder system for medication management. The same reasoning still applies. The professional examines the relationship between the user's goal, the technology or strategy being used, and the environment in which the task occurs. A ramp does not help if boxes are stored on it. A hearing loop does not help if staff do not know how to activate it. A communication device does not support healthcare access if providers rush the interaction and do not allow time for the patient to respond. Accessibility requires both usable features and supportive processes.\n\nAnother core idea is that AT may be specialized, mainstream, built in, customized, or improvised. Specialized tools include screen readers, braille displays, dedicated AAC devices, switches, and certain mobility technologies. Mainstream tools can also serve an assistive function. A smartphone camera, voice assistant, calendar reminder, dark mode setting, browser reading view, dictation tool, noise-canceling headphones, or adjustable font setting may remove a barrier for a particular person. Accessibility professionals should avoid creating an artificial wall between 'real' assistive technology and ordinary technology used for access. What matters is the function the tool serves. If it helps a person perceive, understand, navigate, communicate, remember, control, or participate, it may be functioning as AT in that context.\n\nConsultant thinking begins with four questions. First, what is the user trying to do? A tool should always be understood in relation to a task, such as reading a document, completing a form, joining a meeting, moving through a building, communicating a medical need, or learning course material. Second, what barriers does the environment create? Barriers may be technical, physical, communication-based, policy-based, procedural, attitudinal, or instructional. Third, what information or control does the user's AT require? This may include semantic structure, keyboard operability, clear labels, captions, tactile cues, adequate time, compatibility with platform accessibility Application Programming Interfaces (APIs), or staff who understand how to support the interaction. Fourth, what organizational decision would prevent this barrier from recurring? Sustainable accessibility is not only a one-person fix. It may require procurement standards, document templates, caption quality procedures, staff training, design system components, testing protocols, maintenance plans, or feedback channels.\n\nThis lesson should also correct a subtle but damaging assumption: AT success should not depend on heroic user effort. Many disabled people are highly skilled at adapting to broken systems, but professional accessibility should not celebrate workaround culture as if it were equal access. When a user must memorize unlabeled buttons, ask a coworker to read a PDF, use OCR on every document, request captions after every meeting, or explain keyboard access repeatedly to different teams, the organization is transferring labor to the disabled person. AT may make survival possible in that environment, but it does not make the environment accessible. A mature accessibility program reduces unnecessary workarounds by improving the source systems.\n\nFor CPACC and for professional practice, remember the relationship this way: assistive technology supports functional access, accessible design supports compatibility and usability, and organizational accessibility supports consistency over time. AT helps individuals interact with the world. Accessible design makes that interaction possible and efficient. Organizational maturity makes it reliable, expected, and continuously improved. Domain 4 will introduce many technologies, but the deeper skill is learning to analyze the connection among user need, task, technology, environment, and responsibility. That is the difference between naming AT and understanding accessibility."
    },
    {
      "title": "User Experience Spotlight",
      "body": "A person rarely uses assistive technology in the neat, single-device way that course outlines sometimes imply. Real access is usually more layered. A blind student may use a screen reader to move through headings and links, a refreshable braille display to check spelling and punctuation, Optical Character Recognition (OCR) to extract text from an inaccessible scanned Portable Document Format (PDF), a phone camera for quick object recognition, and a human reader or peer when a system failure becomes too time-consuming to solve alone. A person with limited hand movement may dictate long passages with speech recognition, use keyboard shortcuts for precise editing, rely on switch access for navigation, and keep a touchscreen available for a few tasks that work better by touch. A person with low vision may use magnification for diagrams, text-to-speech for long reading, browser zoom for web pages, high contrast settings for fatigue, and a screen reader when visual access becomes inefficient. The professional lesson is simple but easy to forget: do not reduce a person to one tool.\n\nUser experience matters because assistive technology is not only a technical category. It is part of how a person manages energy, time, privacy, independence, communication, and participation. Two users may choose different tools for the same task because their goals are different. One screen reader user may prefer speech because it is fast for scanning a page. Another may prefer braille because the task involves code, numbers, spelling, punctuation, or careful review. One person may use captions because they are Deaf or hard of hearing. Another may use captions because they are in a noisy room, because the speaker has an unfamiliar accent, because the content is technical, or because reading while listening improves comprehension. In this paragraph, Deaf with a capital D refers to cultural and linguistic identity, while deaf with a lowercase d refers more generally to hearing status. The difference must be explained in words because capitalization alone may not provide equivalent understanding for screen reader users, braille users, or anyone listening to the lesson.\n\nAssistive technology choices also change by setting. A person may use one setup at home, another at work, another on a mobile device, and another in public. A screen reader user may rely on keyboard commands on a laptop, gestures on a phone, braille in a meeting, and audio output through one earbud when privacy matters. A person who uses Augmentative and Alternative Communication (AAC) may use a dedicated communication device for complex conversation, a phone-based communication app for quick exchanges, written notes as a backup, and partner-assisted scanning when fatigue or access barriers interfere. A person with a cognitive disability may use reminders, calendars, plain language summaries, read-aloud tools, visual schedules, checklists, and reduced-distraction settings. These are not signs of inconsistency. They are signs of skilled adaptation to different tasks and environments.\n\nThis is why accessibility professionals should be cautious when interpreting user reports. A user saying, 'I use a screen reader,' does not tell you whether they use speech, braille, touch gestures, keyboard commands, custom scripts, high verbosity, low verbosity, or a combination. A user saying, 'I use speech recognition,' does not tell you whether they can use a mouse occasionally, whether they depend on visible labels, whether fatigue changes their accuracy, or whether background noise affects performance. A user saying, 'I need captions,' does not tell you whether captions alone are enough, whether a transcript is also needed, whether speaker identification matters, or whether Communication Access Realtime Translation (CART) is required for live content. The professional response is curiosity, not assumption.\n\nA useful way to analyze user experience is to separate the task from the tool. The task might be reading a policy, submitting a timesheet, joining a video meeting, finding a classroom, purchasing a ticket, completing a medical form, or participating in training. The tool is only one part of the access path. The professional asks: what does the user need to perceive, understand, decide, operate, communicate, or remember in order to complete the task? Then the professional asks whether the environment supports that need. If the user must complete a web form, the environment must provide labels, instructions, required-field indicators, keyboard access, error identification, and a predictable review path. If the user must participate in a live meeting, the environment may need captions, CART, accessible documents, turn-taking practices, microphone discipline, chat accessibility, and enough time for people using AAC or interpreters to participate. If the user must navigate a building, the environment may need clear routes, tactile information, audible and visual cues, staff support, and policies that keep accessible paths usable.\n\nUser experience also includes emotional and social cost. Inaccessible systems often force disabled people to disclose more than they want, ask for help repeatedly, apologize for barriers they did not create, or spend extra time proving that a problem is real. A user may technically complete a task but only after thirty minutes of guessing through unlabeled buttons, converting documents, contacting support, or asking a coworker to intervene. That is not equal access. The fact that assistive technology made the workaround possible does not make the experience accessible. A professional accessibility evaluation should notice effort, delay, dignity, privacy, and independence, not merely whether the task eventually got done.\n\nConsider a training course with slides, videos, quizzes, and discussion boards. What the organization sees may be a modern learning platform. What different users experience may be very different. A screen reader user may encounter slides exported as images with no text alternatives. A keyboard user may be unable to reach the quiz submit button. A Deaf learner may find auto-generated captions that distort technical terms. A learner with a cognitive disability may struggle because instructions are scattered across several pages. A low vision user may be blocked by text that does not reflow when zoomed. Each person may use AT, but the barriers are not located inside the person or the technology. They are located in the interaction between the user, the task, and the environment.\n\nConsultant thinking turns this observation into action. Instead of saying, 'This user has trouble with the platform,' the consultant documents the specific barrier and its source: unlabeled controls prevent screen reader identification; keyboard focus does not reach required controls; captions omit essential spoken information; documents lack structure; instructions are not available at the point of need; zoom causes content loss. Then the consultant recommends improvements that reduce barriers for many people, not just the person who reported the issue. Better document templates help screen reader and braille users. Accurate captions help Deaf and hard of hearing users, multilingual learners, people in noisy environments, and anyone reviewing technical content. Clear instructions help people with cognitive disabilities and people who are new to the task. Predictable keyboard access helps keyboard users, switch users, screen reader users, and power users.\n\nThe user experience spotlight therefore serves as a warning against shallow AT knowledge. Naming tools is not enough. Accessibility professionals must understand how people combine tools, how context changes access needs, how barriers create extra labor, and how organizational choices can either support or undermine participation. The more carefully professionals listen to user experience, the better they become at identifying patterns that technical checklists alone may miss."
    },
    {
      "title": "Real World Examples",
      "body": "Assistive technology appears in nearly every part of daily life, even when organizations do not recognize it. In digital environments, common examples include screen readers, refreshable braille displays, screen magnification, text-to-speech tools, speech recognition, alternative keyboards, switch access, eye tracking, captions, transcripts, reading supports, spelling and grammar supports, simplified interfaces, and built-in accessibility settings on computers and mobile devices. In physical environments, examples may include mobility devices, hearing loops, tactile signage, audible pedestrian signals, communication boards, wayfinding supports, environmental controls, adaptive workstations, and seating or positioning equipment. In communication environments, examples include Augmentative and Alternative Communication (AAC) devices, captioning, Communication Access Realtime Translation (CART), relay services, hearing aids, cochlear implants, visual alerting systems, and plain language supports. The list is broad because access needs are broad.\n\nA screen reader is a useful starting example because it shows the relationship between assistive technology and accessible design. A screen reader presents digital information through speech or braille. It can announce headings, links, buttons, form fields, table structure, error messages, document text, and application controls when that information is provided correctly. But the screen reader does not control whether a web page has meaningful headings, whether a button has an accessible name, whether a dialog traps focus, or whether a PDF contains real text instead of a picture of text. If a user hears 'button, button, button,' the professional should not assume the user lacks skill. The professional should investigate whether the interface exposes useful names, roles, states, and values.\n\nRefreshable braille displays offer another important example. Braille is not merely a different output format for people who cannot hear speech output. It supports spelling, punctuation, mathematics, code, music, language learning, careful editing, and private reading. A blind professional may listen to speech for speed and use braille for precision. A student may use braille to understand the layout of a math expression or to proofread a paper. A DeafBlind user may depend on braille as a primary channel of digital access. This matters because organizations sometimes assume that audio output replaces braille. It does not. Professional accessibility practice should recognize that different output methods support different kinds of understanding.\n\nScreen magnification and low vision tools also demonstrate the need for flexible design. A person with low vision may enlarge text, change contrast, use a larger pointer, reduce glare, invert colors, use a screen reader for long reading, or combine several settings. Barriers appear when content does not reflow, when text becomes clipped at high zoom, when controls disappear off screen, when color contrast is poor, or when instructions rely only on visual location such as 'click the green box on the right.' The problem is not that the user magnified the content. The problem is that the design did not support magnification, contrast adjustment, or nonvisual explanation.\n\nSpeech recognition and voice control show how accessible names affect operability. A user may say a visible button name such as 'click Submit Request.' If the accessible name is different, missing, or hidden behind an icon with no text, the command may fail or activate the wrong control. Voice users may also need efficient keyboard equivalents, predictable focus, and controls that do not require complex drag-and-drop gestures. Speech recognition can be essential for people with mobility disabilities, repetitive strain injuries, temporary injuries, or fatigue. It can also support productivity for people who prefer dictation. For accessibility professionals, the key lesson is that visible labels, accessible names, and interaction design must align.\n\nSwitch access, alternative keyboards, and eye tracking reveal the cost of poor focus order. A switch user may move through controls one at a time. If a page contains unnecessary stops, hidden controls, repeated navigation, or focus traps, the task becomes slow and exhausting. Eye tracking users may need large targets, stable layouts, predictable timing, and alternatives to hover-only interactions. A person using an alternative keyboard may depend on shortcuts, sticky keys, keyguards, or customized layouts. These examples remind professionals that operability is not only about whether a mouse works. A design must support multiple input methods and must not require fine motor precision, simultaneous gestures, or unpredictable timing when alternatives are possible.\n\nCaptions, transcripts, CART, and sign language interpretation belong in the same professional conversation because they support access to spoken and auditory information, but they are not interchangeable. Captions provide text synchronized with video or live audio. Transcripts provide a text version that can be read, searched, translated, or reviewed at a user's pace. CART provides realtime text for live communication. Sign language interpretation provides access for people whose primary language may be a signed language rather than written English. A Deaf participant may need an interpreter for meaningful participation in a complex meeting even if captions are present. A hard of hearing participant may prefer captions. A person with a cognitive disability may use a transcript to review instructions. A multilingual learner may use captions to support comprehension. The professional task is to match the access support to the communication need rather than treating all text alternatives as identical.\n\nHearing technologies provide another example of the relationship between tools and environments. Hearing aids and cochlear implants may amplify or process sound, but they do not guarantee access in noisy rooms, echoing halls, poorly managed meetings, or videos with bad audio. Hearing loops, assistive listening systems, microphones, captions, visual alerts, and communication practices may all be needed. If a meeting room has an assistive listening system but staff do not know how to use it, the feature exists on paper but fails in practice. This is a recurring accessibility pattern: equipment without process is fragile.\n\nAAC systems highlight communication as participation, not charity. AAC may include dedicated speech-generating devices, symbol boards, communication apps, text-based communication, partner-assisted scanning, gestures, or combinations of methods. A person using AAC may need time to compose messages, accessible meeting practices, patient conversation partners, and systems that do not assume instant verbal response. In healthcare, education, employment, and public services, failure to support AAC can directly affect autonomy, consent, safety, and dignity. The professional should not treat communication access as optional courtesy. It is often central to equal participation.\n\nCognitive and learning supports are sometimes overlooked because they may look ordinary. Reminders, calendars, task lists, plain language, reading rulers, text-to-speech, speech-to-text, simplified layouts, predictable navigation, reduced-distraction modes, and step-by-step prompts may all serve an assistive function. These supports can benefit people with learning disabilities, intellectual disabilities, attention-related disabilities, brain injury, mental health disabilities, age-related changes, and temporary stress or fatigue. They also show why mainstream technology can function as AT. A calendar alert may be as important to one user's participation as a screen reader is to another's.\n\nReal-world examples should push learners away from device memorization and toward barrier analysis. A public transit app that works with a screen reader but gives real-time platform changes only through color still creates barriers. A museum that offers an audio guide but no tactile, captioned, or plain language options serves only some visitors. A workplace that buys ergonomic keyboards but requires inaccessible timekeeping software has addressed one barrier while preserving another. A school that provides a student's AAC device but does not train staff to wait for responses has provided equipment without meaningful communication access. A hospital that offers a patient portal but sends scanned image PDFs has not provided accessible information.\n\nThe professional pattern is always the same. Identify the task. Identify the access need. Identify the technology or strategy the person uses. Identify what the environment must provide. Identify where the chain breaks. Then recommend changes that strengthen the system. Sometimes the recommendation is a specific assistive device or accommodation. Often it is a design, procurement, training, document, communication, or policy improvement. Strong accessibility professionals understand both."
    },
    {
      "title": "Professional Perspective",
      "body": "Accessibility professionals do not need to become experts in every assistive technology product, version, command, or brand. That would be impossible, and it would still miss the point. The professional responsibility is to understand how categories of assistive technology interact with environments, what kinds of barriers commonly interfere with those interactions, and how to investigate problems without blaming the user or the tool too quickly. A consultant who understands the relationship between user need, task, technology, and environment can reason through unfamiliar situations. A consultant who has only memorized device names becomes lost as soon as the product changes.\n\nProfessional perspective begins with humility. Disabled users are often the experts in their own access strategies. They may know exactly which tasks work, which tasks fail, which workarounds are tolerable, and which barriers create serious risk. An accessibility professional should not dismiss that knowledge because a quick automated test passed or because a vendor claimed compatibility. At the same time, user expertise should not be exploited as free quality assurance. The organization remains responsible for building, buying, configuring, and maintaining accessible systems. The user's report is evidence, not a substitute for professional review.\n\nWhen investigating an assistive technology issue, begin by defining the task. 'The screen reader does not work' is too broad to guide remediation. Better questions include: Can the user find the page heading? Can the user identify the purpose of each control? Can the user operate the menu by keyboard? Can the user complete the form? Are errors announced and associated with the correct fields? Can the user review entered information before submitting? Can the user recover from mistakes? These questions turn a vague complaint into a testable access path. The same approach works outside screen reader testing. For captions, ask whether spoken content, speaker changes, sound cues, and timing are accurate enough for the task. For switch access, ask whether focus order and activation patterns support efficient completion. For AAC, ask whether the interaction allows enough time and respects the person's communication method.\n\nA mature professional also separates compatibility from usability. A product may technically expose information to an assistive technology and still be exhausting, confusing, or inefficient. A web page with fifty focus stops before the main task may be technically keyboard reachable but practically burdensome. A form may have labels but place instructions after the fields where users encounter them too late. A video may have captions but include errors that change meaning. A PDF may contain text but have no headings, poor reading order, and tables that do not identify headers. Compatibility asks whether AT can interact with the content at all. Usability asks whether the person can complete the task with reasonable effort, accuracy, dignity, and independence.\n\nAccessibility consultants should also understand the danger of single-tool testing. Testing a website with one screen reader in one browser on one operating system may reveal important barriers, but it does not prove access for all assistive technology users. Screen readers differ. Browsers differ. Mobile and desktop interaction differ. Speech recognition, switch access, magnification, keyboard-only navigation, captions, and cognitive supports reveal different issues. This does not mean every project can test every tool in every combination. It means professionals should be honest about test coverage, avoid overclaiming, and design testing strategies based on risk, audience, platform, and task importance. A banking task, healthcare form, employment application, emergency alert, or required school platform carries higher consequences than a low-risk informational page.\n\nProfessional practice also requires distinguishing the location of responsibility. If a user's assistive technology is misconfigured, training or technical support may be appropriate. If the product lacks labels, keyboard support, captions, or document structure, remediation belongs to the product, content, or process owner. If an organization bought an inaccessible platform without checking accessibility, procurement needs improvement. If staff upload inaccessible documents because they were never trained, the training and template system needs improvement. If support teams tell users to switch browsers without investigating, customer service procedures need improvement. Assistive technology issues often reveal organizational process failures. The consultant's job is to follow the smoke back to the fire.\n\nProcurement is a major professional concern in Domain 4. Organizations frequently assume that if a vendor claims accessibility, the product will work with assistive technology. Strong practice requires more. Procurement teams may request an Accessibility Conformance Report (ACR), often based on a Voluntary Product Accessibility Template (VPAT), but they must also know how to evaluate whether the claims are specific, current, complete, and supported by testing. An ACR that says 'supports' without explaining limitations may not be enough. A product demo should include keyboard use, screen reader behavior where relevant, captions or media access, document output, error handling, and workflows that reflect real tasks. Procurement decisions should include accessibility requirements in contracts, timelines for remediation, support expectations, and consequences for unresolved barriers.\n\nTraining and support are equally important. Assistive technology compatibility can fail after launch because content authors, help desk staff, instructors, managers, or communications teams do not know their role. A beautifully accessible content management system can still produce inaccessible pages if authors skip headings and alternative text. A meeting platform can support captions, but meetings remain inaccessible if hosts do not enable them or if speakers refuse to use microphones. A workplace can approve a screen reader accommodation, but productivity still suffers if internal documents are scanned images. Accessibility professionals should look beyond the tool and ask whether people in the organization understand the practices that keep access working.\n\nProfessional recommendations should be practical and prioritized. It is not enough to say, 'Make this accessible.' A stronger recommendation identifies the barrier, the affected users, the task impact, the likely source, and the corrective action. For example: 'The project status form communicates required fields only through red text. Add programmatic required-field indicators, include text instructions, and ensure errors are announced and linked to the relevant fields. This supports screen reader users, braille users, color blind users, and users with cognitive disabilities who need clear error recovery.' That recommendation connects the technical fix to user impact and organizational value. It teaches while it remediates.\n\nConsultant thinking also includes respect for disability identity and user choice. Professionals should not treat assistive technology as evidence that a person is broken or unusually difficult to serve. AT is part of human variation and participation. People use tools because environments are diverse, bodies are diverse, communication is diverse, and tasks are diverse. The goal is not to make disabled people use technology in a way that is convenient for the organization. The goal is to design and support environments where different access methods are expected and respected.\n\nThe professional perspective for this lesson can be summarized as a shift from gadget thinking to systems thinking. Gadget thinking asks, 'What device does the person use?' Systems thinking asks, 'What is the person trying to do, what access method supports that task, what must the environment provide, where is the barrier, and what organizational change will prevent the same barrier from recurring?' That shift is the difference between shallow awareness and professional accessibility practice. It is also the foundation for the rest of Domain 4."
    },
    {
      "title": "Accessibility Implications",
      "body": "Accessibility implications begin with a practical truth: assistive technology can only work with the information and interaction pathways that the environment makes available. In digital accessibility, that often means semantic structure, keyboard access, programmatic labels, clear instructions, error identification, logical reading order, visible focus, captions, transcripts, and compatibility with platform accessibility Application Programming Interfaces (APIs). In physical and communication environments, it may mean accessible routes, tactile cues, assistive listening systems, communication time, trained staff, plain language materials, reachable controls, and policies that keep accessibility features usable. The specific details change by setting, but the professional reasoning remains consistent. Access is created by the relationship among the user, the technology or strategy, the task, and the environment.\n\nOne major implication is that accessibility cannot be outsourced to the user's assistive technology. A screen reader user should not have to guess the purpose of unlabeled controls. A keyboard user should not have to escape a focus trap by refreshing the browser. A Deaf participant should not have to reconstruct missing information from inaccurate captions. In this sentence, Deaf with a capital D refers to cultural and linguistic identity, while deaf with a lowercase d refers more generally to hearing status. That distinction is explained in words because capitalization alone may not be available to every screen reader or braille user in a meaningful way. A person using Augmentative and Alternative Communication (AAC) should not be rushed through a healthcare decision because the appointment system assumes instant spoken response. When these failures occur, the barrier is not the person's technology. The barrier is the environment's failure to provide the information, time, structure, or control needed for participation.\n\nThis has direct implications for design. Designers must avoid relying only on visual position, color, shape, animation, sound, or timing to communicate meaning. A red outline around a form field does not help a screen reader user unless the error is also identified in text and associated with the field. A button represented only by an icon may not help a speech recognition user unless the visible label and accessible name support predictable commands. A chart that uses color alone may not communicate categories to color blind users, screen reader users, or braille users. A modal dialog that looks visually centered may still be inaccessible if keyboard focus remains behind it or cannot leave it. Accessible design must communicate purpose, state, relationship, and feedback through more than one channel.\n\nThe implications also extend to development and content authoring. Developers need to use native controls where possible, manage focus intentionally, preserve keyboard operation, expose names and roles, support zoom and reflow, and test workflows rather than isolated components. Content authors need to create structured documents with headings, lists, meaningful link text, table headers, text alternatives, accessible form fields, and real text rather than images of text. Media producers need accurate captions, transcripts, audio description when visual information is necessary to understand the content, and accessible media players. A polished document, elegant interface, or beautiful video is not professionally complete if assistive technology users cannot obtain the same meaning and complete the same task.\n\nProcurement is another critical implication. Organizations often purchase systems before anyone asks whether they support assistive technology or accessible workflows. By the time disabled users report barriers, contracts may be signed, budgets spent, and business processes built around inaccessible tools. Accessibility professionals should encourage organizations to include accessibility requirements before purchase, request and review an Accessibility Conformance Report (ACR), ask vendors for evidence, test real workflows, and write accessibility expectations into contracts. A product that claims support for the Web Content Accessibility Guidelines (WCAG) still needs practical review because conformance statements may be incomplete, outdated, vague, or limited to only part of the product.\n\nSupport and maintenance matter just as much as initial design. Accessibility features often fail because no one owns them after launch. Captions may be available but not enabled. Templates may be accessible but not used. A hearing loop may be installed but staff may not know how to activate it. A document system may support tagged Portable Document Format (PDF) output, but authors may upload untagged scanned files. A website may launch with keyboard access and later lose it when a new component is added. Assistive technology compatibility is therefore not a one-time checkpoint. It is a maintenance responsibility. Organizations need training, governance, quality assurance, feedback channels, and repair processes that treat accessibility barriers as operational issues rather than unusual exceptions.\n\nThere are also implications for testing. Automated tools can identify some technical issues, but they cannot determine whether a workflow makes sense to a screen reader user, whether captions preserve meaning, whether a switch user can complete a task efficiently, or whether instructions support people with cognitive disabilities. Manual testing, assistive technology testing, keyboard testing, document review, media review, and user feedback all contribute different evidence. Professionals should be clear about what was tested, what was not tested, which assistive technologies were used, which platforms were covered, and where uncertainty remains. Overclaiming accessibility after narrow testing can harm users and mislead organizations.\n\nThe deepest implication is organizational responsibility. Assistive technology reveals whether an organization's accessibility commitments are real at the point of use. If a user must request a special workaround for every meeting, every document, every form, or every training course, the organization has not built accessibility into its normal operations. Strong accessibility programs reduce repeated individual burden by improving shared systems. They create accessible templates, accessible procurement practices, captioning procedures, inclusive meeting norms, training for staff, technical standards, and accountability. AT remains essential, but it is supported by an environment that expects disabled participation rather than reacting to it as a surprise.\n\nFor professional practice, the accessibility implication is clear: do not ask whether assistive technology is present and stop there. Ask whether the environment gives that technology what it needs. Ask whether the user can complete the task with reasonable effort, privacy, accuracy, dignity, and independence. Ask whether the organization has learned enough from the barrier to prevent the next person from encountering the same problem. That is the difference between accommodating one moment and building accessibility maturity."
    },
    {
      "title": "Common Misconceptions",
      "body": "A common misconception is that assistive technology solves accessibility. It does not. Assistive technology supports access, but it cannot create structure, labels, captions, keyboard support, plain language, accessible routes, or respectful communication where those things are missing. A screen reader can announce a heading only if the content has a real heading. Speech recognition can activate a control more reliably when the visible label and accessible name match. Captions can communicate speech only when they are accurate and synchronized. A mobility device can support movement only when the route is clear and usable. AT is powerful, but it is not a magical repair crew living inside the user's device.\n\nAnother misconception is that assistive technology users are rare edge cases. This idea leads organizations to treat accessibility as a special request rather than a normal part of design and service delivery. In reality, many people use access technologies, accessibility features, and adaptive strategies permanently, temporarily, or situationally. A person may use captions because they are Deaf or hard of hearing, because they are in a noisy room, because they are learning a language, or because the content is technical. A person may use dictation after an injury, during a flare-up of pain, or as a long-term access method. A person may use zoom because of low vision, fatigue, a small screen, or environmental glare. When organizations design for only the imagined average user, they miss the real range of human participation.\n\nA third misconception is that one disability equals one technology. This creates shallow assumptions such as blind people use screen readers, Deaf people use captions, people with mobility disabilities use wheelchairs, and people with cognitive disabilities need simplified content. Each of those statements may be true for some people, but none is complete. A blind person may use speech, braille, magnification, tactile graphics, or human assistance depending on the task. A Deaf person may use sign language interpretation, captions, Communication Access Realtime Translation (CART), written notes, visual alerts, or a combination. A person with a mobility disability may use a wheelchair, cane, prosthetic, speech recognition, switch access, ergonomic equipment, or no visible device at all. A person with a cognitive disability may need memory supports, predictable navigation, plain language, reduced distraction, extra time, or step-by-step instructions. Professional thinking must leave room for individual variation.\n\nA fourth misconception is that testing with one assistive technology proves accessibility. Testing with one screen reader can reveal barriers, but it does not prove that every screen reader, browser, operating system, input method, magnification setting, captioning need, or cognitive access need is supported. Testing with keyboard alone is essential, but it does not replace screen reader testing. Automated testing is useful, but it cannot evaluate whether error recovery makes sense, whether captions preserve meaning, or whether a workflow imposes unreasonable effort. Strong accessibility evaluation uses multiple kinds of evidence and stays honest about scope. A professional does not say, 'This is accessible for everyone,' after a narrow test. A professional says what was tested, what passed, what failed, and what still requires review.\n\nAnother misconception is that built-in accessibility features eliminate the need for accessibility work. Modern operating systems include screen readers, magnification, dictation, captions, contrast settings, pointer adjustments, switch control, and other valuable features. These features matter. They expand access and reduce the need for separate specialized tools. But built-in features still depend on accessible content, accessible applications, and accessible processes. A built-in screen reader cannot make an unlabeled custom control meaningful. A built-in caption feature may help with audio, but it may not replace human-edited captions for technical, legal, medical, or educational content where accuracy matters. Built-in accessibility is part of the ecosystem, not permission to ignore design responsibility.\n\nA related misconception is that assistive technology is always expensive, specialized, and separate from mainstream technology. Some AT is specialized and costly, such as certain braille displays, eye tracking systems, custom seating, or dedicated communication devices. Other AT is free, low cost, built into common devices, or created through ordinary tools used in an access-supporting way. Calendar reminders, browser reader modes, phone cameras, noise-canceling headphones, adjustable fonts, voice assistants, and text-to-speech can all function as assistive technology in context. The professional question is not whether the tool looks medical, specialized, or official. The question is whether it helps the person perceive, understand, communicate, navigate, control, remember, or participate.\n\nSome organizations also believe that if a user has an accommodation, the organization has met its accessibility responsibility. This is a narrow and risky view. An accommodation may be necessary for an individual, but it does not replace accessible systems. If every blind employee needs a separate workaround for the same inaccessible software, the organization should address the software. If every student using captions must request them course by course, the institution should improve media procedures. If every patient using Augmentative and Alternative Communication (AAC) must educate staff during appointments, the healthcare provider should train staff and adjust communication protocols. Individual accommodation and systemic accessibility should support each other. They should not be treated as competitors.\n\nThere is also a misconception that assistive technology use is always obvious. Many disabilities are not visible, and many access strategies are quiet. A person may use captions, reminders, plain language, text-to-speech, keyboard shortcuts, contrast settings, or anxiety-management routines without anyone noticing. A person may not bring a mobility device every day. A person may use speech recognition at home but avoid it in public because of privacy. A person may choose not to disclose a disability at all. Professionals should not wait until they can see disability before designing accessibly. Accessibility should be available without requiring people to prove they need it.\n\nAnother damaging misconception is that user difficulty means user incompetence. When a person struggles with an inaccessible interface, support teams may suggest more training, a different browser, a different assistive technology, or help from a coworker before investigating the design. Training can be useful, and technology configuration can matter, but those possibilities should not become excuses to ignore barriers. If multiple users cannot identify controls, complete forms, access documents, or participate in meetings, the organization should treat those reports as evidence. Even when only one user reports the issue, the barrier may still be real. Accessibility professionals should investigate with respect rather than skepticism as the default posture.\n\nA final misconception is that compliance alone guarantees usable access. Laws, standards, and guidelines are essential, but they are tools for accountability and improvement, not the full measure of human experience. A product may satisfy some technical requirements and still create confusing, slow, or burdensome experiences. A document may have tags but poor reading order. A meeting may have captions but poor microphone discipline. A form may have labels but unclear instructions. Professional accessibility practice uses compliance as a floor, then asks whether people can actually participate. The strongest professionals do not stop at 'Does it meet the rule?' They also ask, 'Does it work for the person, in the task, in the real environment, with dignity?'\n\nCorrecting these misconceptions prepares learners for the rest of Domain 4. Assistive technology is not a substitute for accessibility. Disability does not predict one tool. One test does not prove universal access. Built-in features do not remove design responsibility. Accommodation does not replace systemic improvement. Compliance does not end professional judgment. When learners internalize these distinctions, they begin to think less like device catalogers and more like accessibility professionals."
    },
    {
      "title": "CPACC Exam Focus",
      "body": "For the Certified Professional in Accessibility Core Competencies (CPACC) examination, understand assistive technology as part of functional access. The exam may ask about categories of AT, but the deeper concept is the relationship among disability, task, environment, technology, and participation. AT is not defined only by expensive equipment or specialized devices. It includes hardware, software, built-in accessibility features, services, supports, and strategies that help people perform tasks, communicate, access information, control environments, and participate. A phone reminder, screen reader, braille display, captioning service, hearing loop, speech recognition tool, communication device, magnifier, switch, or reading support may all function as AT depending on the user's need and context.\n\nBe ready to distinguish assistive technology from accessible design. Assistive technology is used by or for an individual to support access. Accessible design reduces barriers in the product, service, information, communication, or environment itself. These concepts work together, but they are not interchangeable. A screen reader helps a blind user access digital content, but the website still needs headings, labels, keyboard support, logical focus order, and meaningful text alternatives. Captions support access to audio information, but media workflows still need accurate caption creation and quality review. A wheelchair supports mobility, but the built environment still needs accessible routes, clear space, ramps, elevators, door hardware, and policies that keep routes usable.\n\nAlso be ready to distinguish assistive technology from accommodations and universal design. An accommodation is an adjustment for an individual or a specific situation, such as providing a particular device, interpreter, captioning service, alternate format, schedule modification, or support person. Universal design aims to make environments usable by as many people as possible from the beginning. AT may be part of an accommodation, and universal design may reduce the need for some accommodations, but neither concept erases the other. A professional understands how they interact. For example, an accessible online training platform is a universal design improvement, while a specific braille display for an employee may be an accommodation. Both may be necessary.\n\nThe CPACC exam may include examples that test whether you can identify the barrier rather than blame the user's technology. If a screen reader announces several controls only as 'button,' the likely issue is missing accessible names or labels. If a keyboard user cannot move out of a dialog, the issue may be focus management or keyboard trapping. If a person using speech recognition cannot activate a control by saying its visible name, the issue may be a mismatch between visible text and the programmatic accessible name. If a video includes captions that omit important instructions, the barrier is caption quality, not the user's hearing status. If a scanned Portable Document Format (PDF) cannot be read by a screen reader, the issue may be missing real text, Optical Character Recognition (OCR), tags, structure, or reading order.\n\nKnow common categories of assistive technology, but do not study them as isolated vocabulary. Screen readers and refreshable braille displays support nonvisual access to digital information. Screen magnification, contrast adjustments, and display customization support many low vision users. Speech recognition, alternative keyboards, switches, eye tracking, and sip-and-puff systems support different input and control needs. Captions, transcripts, Communication Access Realtime Translation (CART), assistive listening systems, hearing aids, cochlear implants, relay services, visual alerts, and sign language interpretation support access to auditory and spoken communication in different ways. Augmentative and Alternative Communication (AAC) systems support expression and interaction for people who do not rely on speech alone. Cognitive supports may include reminders, plain language, task prompts, text-to-speech, simplified interfaces, and organizational tools.\n\nRemember that AT use is individualized. The exam may describe a person with a disability and ask which support is appropriate, but professional reasoning should avoid assuming that a disability label automatically determines one tool. Two people with the same disability may use different technologies. One person may use multiple tools. A person may use different tools in different environments. A person may use mainstream technology as AT. The best answer usually focuses on the functional need and the task rather than on a stereotype about the disability category.\n\nBe prepared for questions about compatibility. Assistive technologies often rely on information exposed by software, documents, devices, or environments. In digital contexts, this includes accessible names, roles, states, values, semantic headings, form labels, table structure, focus order, keyboard operability, captions, transcripts, and compatibility with accessibility APIs. API stands for Application Programming Interface. In accessibility, platform accessibility APIs help software communicate information to assistive technologies. If that information is missing or inaccurate, AT may not be able to present or operate the content correctly.\n\nThe exam may also test awareness that accessibility features can benefit people beyond permanent disability categories. Captions may help Deaf and hard of hearing users, language learners, people in noisy environments, and people reviewing technical content. Voice input may help people with mobility disabilities, temporary injuries, fatigue, or productivity needs. Magnification and contrast settings may help low vision users, older adults, mobile users, or people working in glare. This does not mean disability access should be justified only by convenience for others. It means accessibility often improves participation across a wide range of human situations.\n\nFor CPACC purposes, use precise language. Assistive technology supports access, but inaccessible design can still block AT users. Accessibility features built into mainstream devices may function as AT when used for access. Accommodations may include AT, but organizational accessibility should reduce unnecessary reliance on individual workarounds. Compliance standards matter, but professional judgment must consider user impact, task completion, and participation. When an answer choice blames the user or the user's device without examining the environment, be cautious. CPACC expects candidates to understand disability as an interaction between people and barriers, not as a defect located only in the individual.\n\nThe safest exam strategy is to ask: What is the person trying to do? What barrier is present? What access need is involved? What technology or support could help? What should the environment provide so that the technology or support can work? This reasoning connects Domain 4 to earlier course themes: disability models, diversity, universal design, inclusive design, and accessible information. The exam may use device names, but the professional concept is participation."
    },
    {
      "title": "Lesson Summary",
      "body": "Assistive technology is one part of the larger accessibility ecosystem. It includes tools, devices, software, services, built-in features, and strategies that help people access information, communicate, move through environments, control technology, manage tasks, and participate in daily life. Assistive Technology (AT) may be specialized, mainstream, customized, temporary, permanent, high-tech, low-tech, or improvised. What makes something assistive is not its price, appearance, or label. What matters is the access function it serves for a particular person in a particular context.\n\nThe central lesson is that AT and accessible design are partners, not substitutes. A screen reader, braille display, captioning service, switch system, speech recognition tool, magnifier, hearing technology, communication device, or reminder system can be powerful, but it still depends on the surrounding environment. Digital systems need structure, labels, keyboard access, error handling, focus management, captions, transcripts, and compatibility with accessibility Application Programming Interfaces (APIs). Documents need real text, headings, reading order, table structure, and meaningful alternatives for nontext content. Physical environments need accessible routes, usable controls, clear wayfinding, communication supports, and maintained accessibility features. Organizational environments need training, procurement standards, support procedures, feedback channels, and accountability.\n\nThis lesson also emphasized that assistive technology use is individual. One disability label does not predict one tool. A blind person may use speech, braille, magnification, Optical Character Recognition (OCR), tactile information, or a combination. A Deaf person may use captions, sign language interpretation, Communication Access Realtime Translation (CART), visual alerts, written communication, or other supports. Deaf with a capital D refers to cultural and linguistic identity, while deaf with a lowercase d refers more generally to hearing status. A person with a mobility disability may use wheelchairs, alternative keyboards, speech recognition, switches, eye tracking, or ergonomic supports. A person with a cognitive disability may use reminders, plain language, text-to-speech, predictable layouts, or step-by-step prompts. Professional accessibility practice begins with user goals and task demands, not stereotypes.\n\nThe lesson introduced the difference between gadget thinking and systems thinking. Gadget thinking asks only what device a person uses. Systems thinking asks what the person is trying to do, what barriers the environment creates, what information or control the assistive technology requires, and what organizational change would prevent the barrier from recurring. This shift matters because many accessibility failures are not caused by the absence of AT. They are caused by inaccessible design, weak procurement, poor document practices, inaccurate media access, untrained staff, inaccessible workflows, or maintenance failures.\n\nA strong accessibility professional does not treat disabled users as the problem to be solved. The professional investigates the interaction among the person, task, technology, environment, and organization. If a user hears 'button' with no meaningful label, the professional examines the interface. If a user cannot operate a dialog by keyboard, the professional examines focus management. If captions omit important spoken content, the professional examines caption quality and media workflow. If a communication device user is rushed, the professional examines meeting, healthcare, or service procedures. If a user repeatedly needs workarounds, the professional asks how the system can be improved for everyone who follows.\n\nFor the Certified Professional in Accessibility Core Competencies (CPACC) examination, remember that AT supports functional access, but inaccessible environments can still block participation. Be able to identify common categories of AT, distinguish AT from accessible design and accommodations, and analyze how tools interact with barriers. For professional practice, go further. Listen to user experience. Respect user choice. Avoid assumptions. Document barriers clearly. Recommend practical improvements. Connect individual access issues to organizational systems.\n\nThe purpose of Domain 4 is not to memorize a museum shelf of devices. The purpose is to understand how people use technology and strategies to participate, and how accessibility professionals can make environments more compatible, usable, respectful, and sustainable. Assistive technology can create access, but only when the route, the control, the instructions, the instructions, the timing, and the people responsible for the space are also part of the accessibility plan."
    }
  ]
};
